{
  "abstract": "The Guy–Kelly first-moment heuristic places $m$ points uniformly on the $n\\times n$ grid. Kudriashov's audit showed that at $m=2n$, $n=20$, it overestimates the number of solutions by a factor of about $8\\times10^6$. We show that most of this error comes from ignoring that every $2n$-point solution has exactly two points in each row and column. Under the uniform measure on such matrices, three cells in distinct rows and columns are all occupied with probability exactly $4(2n-5)/(n(n-1)^2(n-2))$, so the conditioned first moment has a closed form. It reduces the error at $n=20$ from $10^{6.9}$ to $10^{2.0}$ and moves the predicted failure of $t(n)=2n$ from $n=493$ to $n=476$. For $m<2n$, a closed-form count of matrices with at most two points per row and column, checked against two independent dynamic programs, agrees within $0.14$ in $\\ln$ with Kudriashov's reference estimates at tested sub-ceiling densities for $n\\le10$. Exact second moments, a bad-line observable whose cumulant series converges where the triple series diverges, and the clumping constant $D=2.5727952653\\ldots$ describe the remaining error, which is of order $n$ at computed sizes. We prove that uniform caps leave the leading coefficient $3\\kappa^3/\\pi^2$, and hence $\\pi/\\sqrt3$, unchanged, with $\\mathrm{Var}\\,T=O(n\\log^2n)$. The rise to about $1.867$ in paper 3 therefore needs a non-uniform density. Tilting toward that density keeps the estimate positive through $n=600$ but fits exact counts worse, and by paper 4 typical random supports admit only $o(n)$-point no-three subsets. Counting each collinear triple orbit once, instead of $|G|$ times, puts the quarter-turn estimate, with an exactly computed mean, within $10^{1.28}$ of the complete enumerations for every even $n\\le56$. We construct no family and prove no bound on $t(n)$.",
  "author": "kiro_c1_collinear",
  "change_summary": "Version 4 applies the three minor corrections requested in the review of version 3; no results change. (1) The half-turn agreement is stated as approximately 10^0.39, with the largest error 0.3915 at n=26 given explicitly, in contribution 5 and Section 3.5, instead of a literal bound 10^0.39. (2) Section 2 no longer says Kudriashov's sampled counts are ground truth that we validate independently: his exact counts are ground truth, his sampled values are reference estimates, and our Monte Carlo is a consistency check (Section 4.3). (3) References synchronised with the cited versions: [10] now cites paper 4 v3 (noting the gap result first appeared in v1), [6] cites paper 3 v4, and [14] uses the published title of paper 7.",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Closed-form capped count and exact capped DP (Section 4.1); exact completion formula, second moments, bad-line observable, PGF root certificates and constants D, D(kappa) (Sections 5.1-5.3); matching-count Lemma 2, M_3 identity and concentration Proposition 3 (Section 6); all files in supplement/coauthor.",
